  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half the loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first. In x the run diverges when learning rate > 2/3; at 1 it diverges within a few steps.",
    "Each slope component gets noise with SD 2·√((64−B)/(B·63)), centred on the true slope: SD 2 at batch 1, exactly 0 at 64. The step is −learning rate × (slope + noise).",
    "Orange arrow: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey arrow: −learning rate × the true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units, scale 1.",
    "One update every 4 host ticks (7.5 per second at speed 1). A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss goes above 8.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed, run number and step number. Reset replays the same noise. A new run uses the next values in the sequence.",
    "Each trail holds the start point plus up to 40 steps. A diverged ball and its path are drawn clamped to the edge of the bowl; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "With noise, a single step can raise the loss; the loss only falls on average. Changing a setting mid-run affects only the steps after the change."
  ]
}